/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Mains has two wires, live and neutral, and a third protective earth wire.
   - Class II equipment has double insulation: basic insulation plus a second layer, or reinforced insulation, so no protective earth is needed.
   - An applied part touches the patient.
   - An isolation transformer separates the device supply from the earthed mains, so the secondary has no reference to earth.
   - Enclosure leakage current flows from the metal case to earth through a person touching the case.
   - Iearth is protective-earth-conductor leakage; Ienclosure is case-to-earth leakage; Ipatient is patient leakage; B, BF and CF are applied-part types.
   - The analyser does not use a plain ammeter.
   - The earth wire resistance limit is ≤ 0.1 Ω from the earth pin to the enclosure, 0.2 Ω with a detachable mains cord; routine hospital tests that include the cord commonly accept up to 0.3 Ω.
   - For micro-shock, use isolated patient inputs, keep the patient circuit away from earthed metal, and during catheter procedures ask staff to work with one hand in the pocket.
   - A safety analyser is a meter with a mains outlet, a test load and switches for normal and single-fault conditions.
*/
