/* book exam traps: Testing only in normal condition: a device can pass normal leakage and fail with the earth open. The single-fault test is the real protection. | Mixing resistance units: 0.1 Ω is an earth-wire resistance, while 1 MΩ is an insulation resistance. Do not compare them as if they were the same test. | Rejecting class II equipment for missing a protective earth: class II uses double insulation and does not need an earth wire, but it must still pass insulation and enclosure leakage. | Comparing patient leakage with earth leakage: 100 µA is the patient limit for B or BF; 5 mA is the earth-conductor leakage limit; the enclosure (case) leakage limit is 100 µA in normal condition. Do not use the wrong number. | Reversing type CF and type BF limits: type CF is stricter at 10 µA, not looser than the 100 µA limit for B or BF. */
